---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Percentage calculator: percent of, percent change, and what percent"
  description="Three percentage sums on one page: what is X% of Y, X is what percent of Y, and the percentage increase or decrease between two numbers. Each with its working shown."
>
  <h1>Percentage calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Nearly every percentage question is one of three sums. This page holds all three, with the
    answer sentence written out in words, because "25%" on its own rarely says which thing it
    is 25% of.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--money"><img src="/img/percent.webp" alt="Red sale tags on a dark background" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>
    <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#the-three-sums">The three sums</a></li><li><a href="#the-three-formulas">The three formulas</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <div class="sheet">
    <h2 id="the-three-sums" style="margin-top:0;">The three sums</h2>
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="a1">What is this percent</label>
        <input type="number" id="a1" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="15" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="a2">of this number?</label>
        <input type="number" id="a2" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="240" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="result-figure" id="out-a" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="b1">This number</label>
        <input type="number" id="b1" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="45" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="b2">is what percent of this?</label>
        <input type="number" id="b2" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="180" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="result-figure" id="out-b" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="c1">From this number</label>
        <input type="number" id="c1" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="80" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="c2">to this number is what change?</label>
        <input type="number" id="c2" inputmode="decimal" step="any" value="100" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="result-figure" id="out-c" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-three-formulas">The three formulas</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Formula</th><th scope="col" class="num">Example</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What is 15% of 240?</td><td>percent ÷ 100 × number</td><td class="num">36</td></tr>
      <tr><td>45 is what percent of 180?</td><td>part ÷ whole × 100</td><td class="num">25%</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>80 up to 100 is what change?</td><td>(new − old) ÷ old × 100</td><td class="num">+25%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The third one is where people get caught. The change is always measured against where you
    started, never against where you land. Going from 80 to 100 is a 25% increase, and going
    back from 100 to 80 is only a 20% decrease. That is why a price that falls 50% then rises
    50% does not end up back where it started: it ends 25% down.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>What is the difference between percent and percentage points?</dt>
    <dd>
      If an interest rate goes from 4% to 5%, it rose by one percentage point, which is a 25%
      increase in the rate itself. News reports mix the two constantly. When the difference
      matters, use points for the gap between two percents and percent for the change.
    </dd>
    <dt>How do I add a percentage to a number, like a tip or tax?</dt>
    <dd>
      Multiply by one plus the percent as a decimal. A bill of 84 with 15% added is 84 × 1.15
      = 96.60. To take 20% off, multiply by 0.80. Or use the first sum on this page to find
      the percent part and add it on.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why can a decrease not be more than 100%?</dt>
    <dd>
      Because a 100% decrease means the whole thing is gone. If a calculator anywhere tells
      you something fell by 150%, it measured against the wrong end. Increases have no such
      limit: going from 10 to 35 is a 250% increase.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: the three standard percentage formulas, change always measured from the starting value. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);
    const num = (n) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { maximumFractionDigits: 4 });

    function compute() {
      const a1 = parseFloat($('a1').value);
      const a2 = parseFloat($('a2').value);
      $('out-a').textContent = Number.isFinite(a1) && Number.isFinite(a2)
        ? num(a1) + '% of ' + num(a2) + ' is ' + num(a1 / 100 * a2)
        : '';
      const b1 = parseFloat($('b1').value);
      const b2 = parseFloat($('b2').value);
      $('out-b').textContent = Number.isFinite(b1) && Number.isFinite(b2) && b2 !== 0
        ? num(b1) + ' is ' + num(b1 / b2 * 100) + '% of ' + num(b2)
        : '';
      const c1 = parseFloat($('c1').value);
      const c2 = parseFloat($('c2').value);
      if (Number.isFinite(c1) && Number.isFinite(c2) && c1 !== 0) {
        const ch = (c2 - c1) / c1 * 100;
        $('out-c').textContent = num(c1) + ' to ' + num(c2) + ' is a ' + num(Math.abs(ch)) + '% ' + (ch >= 0 ? 'increase' : 'decrease');
      } else {
        $('out-c').textContent = '';
      }
    }

    for (const id of ['a1', 'a2', 'b1', 'b2', 'c1', 'c2']) $(id).addEventListener('input', compute);
    compute();
  </script>
</Base>
